\title{The Bridge of My Hometown}
\author{Wanzhou Lin}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The Bridge in Memory}
The stone bridge of my hometown spans a narrow river,
its body laid from blue-grey slabs, its balustrade carved
with lotus patterns worn smooth by the years.
As a child I would sit beneath its arch and watch the fishing
boats pass through, the boatwomen's work songs drifting far
along the water.
On summer nights the neighbours would gather at the bridgehead
to enjoy the cool air, talking of the harvest and of relatives
in distant towns.

\section{The Bridge Gone}
When I returned the year before last, the stone bridge was gone.
In its place stood a flat concrete crossing:
wide, level, and silent.
The river still runs its course, but no one sits at the
bridgehead any more, and the work songs beneath the arch
have vanished together with those summer-evening chats.

\end{document}

